Bowel Obstruction (Malignant)
Palliative & End of Life
Colicky diffuse abdominal pain, Absolute constipation/obstipation, Feculent vomiting

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PEL-014
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe colicky abdominal pain and profuse, foul-smelling vomiting preventing any oral intake.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported the sudden onset of severe, unremitting colicky abdominal pain associated with profound, forceful feculent vomiting and absolute constipation over the last 3 days. Urgent after-hours assessment was mandatory to diagnose the suspected malignant bowel obstruction, provide immediate parenteral symptom relief, and prevent severe dehydration and aspiration prior to arranging specialist intervention in the morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My stomach is cramping in waves of absolute agony, and I can't stop vomiting up this foul, brown liquid."
History of Present Illness: The patient has a known history of definitively advanced intra-abdominal pelvic malignancy. Over the past 72 hours, the patient has developed absolute, intractable, unremitting constipation entirely halting all passage of flatus and stool. This evening, they experienced the sudden onset of severe, episodic, strictly colicky diffuse abdominal pain directly corresponding with uncoordinated bowel peristalsis. This was rapidly followed by profound, voluminous, and forcefully recurrent bilious and now overtly feculent vomiting. The spouse notes a severely, globally distended abdomen. There is rapid, profound systemic dehydration secondary to massive third-spacing and continuous emesis.
Functional Status: Bedbound due to severe pain and weakness; completely dependent for care today.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extremely high risk of aspiration of feculent vomitus; severe caregiver distress watching the patient writhe in pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 90/60 mmHg | HR: 118 bpm | Temp: 37.4 C | RR: 22 bpm | SpO2: 96% on room air | Wt: 60 Kg
General: Patient is profoundly dehydrated, pale, diaphoretic, and actively retching into an emesis bag.
Physical Exam: Abdominal inspection reveals a severely, globally distended and distinctly tympanic abdomen with visible peristalsis across the extremely thin abdominal wall. Auscultation reveals high-pitched, tinkling hyperactive bowel sounds explicitly auscultated directly corresponding with the waves of pain. Palpation elicits diffuse tenderness without rigid peritonitis. The diagnostic tetrad is fulfilled by the observed severe colicky diffuse abdominal pain, the reported absolute constipation/obstipation, and the active feculent vomiting.
Environmental Observations: Multiple soiled emesis bags are present. The environment smells strongly of feculent matter.
Medication Reconciliation: Oral pain medications are entirely ineffective as they are immediately vomited.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Bowel Obstruction (Malignant) - Status: Exacerbated (Acute Complete Obstruction)
Differentials:

Gastroenteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absolute lack of diarrhea or flatus, and the distinct feculent nature of the emesis, which strongly points to an established mechanical block.

Perforated viscus - Ruled out/unlikely at this exact moment due to the absence of a rigid, board-like abdomen and profound septic shock, though the risk remains extremely high.

Severe opioid-induced constipation - Ruled out/unlikely to be the sole cause given the known advanced pelvic malignancy acting as a direct mechanical constricting mass, though opioids likely exacerbated the dysmotility.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, complete malignant bowel obstruction, evidenced by the classic triad of colicky abdominal pain, absolute obstipation, and feculent vomiting. The objective findings of tachycardia, hypotension, and a distended, tinkling abdomen confirm severe third-spacing and mechanical blockage. Because the patient is entirely unable to absorb oral analgesics or antiemetics, and is suffering agonizing colicky spasms with a high risk of fatal aspiration, delaying assessment until normal hours would result in unimaginable suffering and potentially a fatal bowel perforation. Immediate after-hours intervention was absolutely necessary to establish a continuous subcutaneous infusion to halt bowel spasms, dry up secretions, and relieve the agonizing pain.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered stat subcutaneous hyoscine butylbromide (to halt colicky smooth muscle spasms) and subcutaneous haloperidol (for centrally-driven nausea). Prescribed stat subcutaneous morphine for the background pain. Facilitated an urgent referral to the palliative care team to establish a continuous syringe driver.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you feel a wave of vomiting coming on (trigger), you must firmly hold the emesis basin and explicitly turn your head completely to the side (behavior). This will ensure the fluid drains out rather than pooling in your throat, directly preventing you from choking or inhaling the fluid (positive consequence).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent palliative care nursing review required first thing in the morning to set up the syringe driver.
DME/Safety (Optional): Ensure an adequate supply of emesis bags and mouth care sponges.
Follow-up: Palliative care team review tomorrow morning; GP review in 24 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient develops sudden, continuous, severe "tearing" abdominal pain, develops a high fever, or if the vomitus resembles pure, fresh blood, the spouse must call 000 immediately, as this may indicate a catastrophic bowel perforation.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The aim of community management is strictly symptom control (pain and nausea relief). Reversing the actual physical blockage is highly unlikely given the advanced cancer.
Clinical Course: The bowel will likely remain permanently blocked. The goal is to dry up the fluids entering the stomach so the vomiting stops, allowing a peaceful passing.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to force the patient to eat or drink anything, as it will only increase the vomiting. Call the after-hours doctor again if the severe cramps return before the morning nurses arrive.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The tumor has completely blocked the bowel. Because fluid cannot go down, it is coming back up. The injections we gave will stop the painful squeezing of the bowel and dry up the stomach juices to stop the vomiting.
Actionable Steps: Nil by mouth except for tiny ice chips or mouth swabs purely for comfort. Spit out any saliva rather than swallowing it to reduce the fluid load in the stomach.

